 "observations": [
  "The page number «122» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 11», in bold sans serif, underlined. The titles (L01, L02), in bold, the sigla in roman: L01 that of C and O, L02 that of C and N; the folios of the manuscripts, «C 49r-v, O 105v,» over «N 28v.», stand at the left of the titles, the letters raised; here as left margin notes, divided after «C 49r-v,» so that they keep within the page. The page has no signature.",
  "The table: a thick and a thin rule above the head, a double rule below it; vertical rules divide the fifteen columns, double ones before and after the columns of the years and of the months; thin rules under the heads that span the four columns of signs, degrees, minutes and seconds, whose heads «Sig.», «Gr.», «Min.», «Sec.» are set in small bold type. «Anni collecti» is printed turned through a quarter, reading upwards, in two lines, «Anni plani» and «Menses arabum4)» in two lines; here their words stand in L03 and L04. Each printed row is one line here, the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. The collected years end with 570 (L25).",
  "L06-L35, the right-hand block: the months fill the first twelve rows (L06-L17); below them a thick and a thin rule, a second head, «Dies et horae» and «Heza Jovis praedictum18)» over the four columns, without heads of the signs, degrees, minutes and seconds, set between the rows 14 and 15 (here L20), a double rule, and the anomaly of Jupiter in days and hours (L22-L33); the last two rows are empty.",
  "L06-L35, the years and months. Checked against Brahmagupta's parameter, 364 226 455 revolutions of Jupiter in 1 577 916 450 000 days, a daily mean motion of 4′ 59″ 9‴ 9⁗, 5s 13° 24′ 49.1″ in 30 Arab years (10 631 days): from the radix 11s 0° 16′ 49″ (330° 16′ 49″) the collected years agree to within 2″ (with the readings of notes a-e); the 30 single years, 354 days and 355 in the leap years 2, 5, 7, 10, 13, 16, 18, 21, 24, 26 and 29, agree to within 0.2″ (with the readings of notes f and g); the months, 30, 59, … 354 days, agree to within 0.5″ (with the reading of note i), the year of the months, 29° 25′ 0″, being the single year 1.",
  "L22-L33, the anomaly («Heza») of Jupiter, the excess of the sun's mean motion over Jupiter's (p. 56, JP2 0088): 54′ 9″ 1‴ a day with Brahmagupta's parameters. Checked: the days 1, 5, 10, 15, 20, 30 and 60 and the hours 1, 3, 6 and 12 agree with it to within 1.2″ (with the readings of notes h and k). The 25 days are printed 22° 53′ 45″, against 22° 33′ 45.5″ computed (between 18° 3′ 0″ and 27° 4′ 30″, each 5 days 4° 30′ 45″): the minutes are 20 too large; no note bears on them.",
  "L17, «Dulheia16», and N08, «i] 54]»; N01, «C, 3)», and N02, «Rabe. I.]»: see the critical notes. A speck after «Elmuharram7)» (L06, 3 px) and one before «Min.» in the head of the single years (L05); not transcribed.",
  "N02: «Elm̃.», N03-N04: «Gum̃.», N04: «Răg̃», «Scaw̃», with the tildes and the breve as printed; «Rāthan» with the macron. N02: «V. notas in tab. 8 —», without a point after the 8. N08: «O N», the two sigla set apart. The ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmata as printed. Italics: «V. notas in tab. 8»."
 ],
